  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Golden ear (Naematelia aurantia) is a gelatinous fungus belonging to the Naemateliaceae family, characterized by its distinctive ear-shaped or brain-like fruiting bodies. This wood-decay fungus is commonly found throughout New England and plays an important ecological role in decomposing dead wood.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous Texture",
      "description": "The fruiting body has a translucent, jelly-like or gelatinous consistency that becomes firm when dry",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Golden-Orange Color",
      "description": "Exhibits a distinctive golden-yellow to bright orange coloration, particularly when moist and fresh",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Pattern",
      "description": "Forms ear-shaped or convoluted, brain-like structures that grow directly on decaying hardwood, especially oak and maple",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Golden ear thrives on dead wood and fallen logs of hardwood trees in moist, shaded forests throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "When dry, golden ear shrinks dramatically and becomes hard and brittle, but it quickly revives and regains its gelatinous texture when exposed to moisture, making it a resilient decomposer through seasonal changes.",
